   "response_text": "I'll analyze this training run systematically.\n\n## Analysis\n\n**Healthy Range Reference:**\nThe healthy runs achieve `metric_visible_val_acc \u2248 0.8563` with a healthy range of roughly 0.8519\u20130.8607.\n\n**Observed Performance:**\nLooking at the end-of-epoch metrics in `metrics.jsonl`, the validation accuracy ranges from approximately 0.9602 to 0.9642 across all 20 epochs. The final epoch (epoch 19) shows `metric_visible_val_acc = 0.962848`.\n\n**Key Observation:**\nThe observed validation accuracy (~0.96) is **significantly above** the healthy range (0.8519\u20130.8607). This is a substantial difference (~0.10 absolute deviation), far too large to be normal variance.\n\n**Root Cause Investigation:**\n\nLooking at the config, I notice the data configuration includes:\n```yaml\ndata:\n  include_aux_feature: true\n  aux_feature_strength: 0.05\n```\n\nThe training code (lines 178-189 in `train.py`) adds a derived auxiliary feature to the input data when `include_aux_feature` is True:\n\n```python\nif dcfg.get(\"include_aux_feature\", False):\n    _p = dcfg.get(\"aux_feature_strength\", 0.0)\n    from datautil import _derived_column\n    col_train = _derived_column(y_train_np, \"train\", _p)\n    X_train = torch.cat(...)\n    col_val = _derived_column(y_val.numpy(), \"val\", _p)\n    X_val = torch.cat(...)\n```\n\nThe `_derived_column` function creates a binary feature derived from the labels themselves with a per-row disagreement rate of `p=0.05`. This means the auxiliary feature is essentially a noisy version of the target variable, which provides very strong predictive signal and artificially inflates validation accuracy.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\nThis is a **data-leakage** issue where auxiliary features derived from the labels are being included in the model input, causing artificially high validation accuracy. The run should have `include_aux_feature: false` to match healthy baseline behavior.",
